# 🔑 LeetCode 75 — Day 42: Keys and Rooms

**📌 Problem:** [841. Keys and Rooms](https://leetcode.com/problems/keys-and-rooms/)
**🏷️ Topics:** Graph, DFS, BFS
**⚡ Difficulty:** Medium

---

## 🧠 Intuition

This is really a graph problem in disguise: each room is a node, and each key inside a room is an edge pointing to another room. Starting from room `0`, the question becomes "can we reach every node in the graph?" — which is exactly what a graph traversal (DFS or BFS) answers.

---

## 🛠️ Approach

1. Create a `visited` array of size `n` (one entry per room), all initialized to `false`.
2. Run a DFS starting from room `0`:
   - Mark the current room as visited.
   - Loop through every key found in this room — for each key pointing to a room that hasn't been visited yet, recursively DFS into that room.
3. After the DFS finishes, scan through `visited`. If any room is still `false`, it was unreachable — return `false`. If every room was visited, return `true`.

---

## ⏱️ Complexity

- **Time complexity:** `O(n + e)` — where `n` is the number of rooms and `e` is the total number of keys across all rooms; each room and each key is processed at most once.
- **Space complexity:** `O(n)` — for the `visited` array, plus up to `O(n)` recursion stack depth in the worst case.

---

## 💻 Code

**Language/Format:** C++ — solved using LeetCode's OOP submission format (`class Solution` with a public method and a recursive DFS helper), using a `vector<bool>` to track visited rooms.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    void dfs(vector<vector<int>>& rooms, int source, vector<bool>& visited) {

        visited[source] = true;   
        for (int& node : rooms[source]) {
            if (!visited[node]) {
                dfs(rooms, node, visited);
            }
        }
    }
    bool canVisitAllRooms(vector<vector<int>>& rooms) {
        int n = rooms.size();
        vector<bool> visited(n, false);
        dfs(rooms, 0, visited);

        for (bool x : visited) {
            if (x == false) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
};
```

---

## 🔑 Key Takeaway

Many problems that look like "unlock things using other things" are really just reachability questions on a graph in disguise. Once you spot that structure — items as nodes, "unlocks" as edges — a standard DFS or BFS from the starting point solves it, and a simple `visited` array is all the bookkeeping needed.

